dev tun
client
proto tcp-client
ca "{{CA}}"
cert "{{CERT}}"
key "{{KEY}}"
{{VERIFY_X509_NAME}}
remote-cert-eku "TLS Web Server Authentication"
remote {{DOMAIN}} 443
persist-key
persist-tun
verb 3
# (no keepalive of our own: Fireboxes always push their own "ping X,
# ping-restart Y" in the PUSH_REPLY and that overrides any value we set
# here -- confirmed in real logs, so declaring it here is pointless.)
cipher AES-256-CBC
data-ciphers AES-256-CBC
auth SHA256
float
reneg-sec 43200
nobind
auth-user-pass
auth-nocache
tls-version-min 1.2
# Transport is forced to TCP (proto tcp-client) because the Firebox only
# accepts SSLVPN on TCP 443 -- there is no UDP option. This means anything
# that's already TCP inside the tunnel (like SSH) ends up "TCP inside TCP",
# a known scenario (TCP-over-TCP meltdown) where the two layers'
# retransmission timers interfere and feel like lag/jitter while typing
# even when raw bandwidth is fine. tcp-nodelay disables Nagle's algorithm
# on the tunnel's TCP socket so OpenVPN doesn't buffer small packets (like
# each SSH keystroke) before sending them -- it doesn't remove the
# underlying issue but noticeably reduces the perceived delay.
tcp-nodelay
# mssfix caps the MSS of TCP flows carried *inside* the tunnel (SSH,
# HTTPS, etc. -- not the outer OpenVPN TCP connection itself) so their
# segments arrive already small enough to survive the tunnel's own
# overhead, instead of relying on Path MTU Discovery. That matters
# specifically on mobile networks/hotspots: many carrier NATs silently
# drop the ICMP "fragmentation needed" replies PMTUD depends on, so an
# oversized segment doesn't get resized -- it just vanishes and waits for
# a full TCP retransmission timeout, which shows up as a multi-hundred-ms
# latency spike (observed on a phone hotspot: 50-300ms baseline jitter
# plus occasional 350-580ms spikes). 1400 is a conservative value that
# leaves headroom for whatever extra encapsulation the mobile carrier's
# own network adds on top. Complements tcp-nodelay above (that one
# addresses buffering/Nagle; this one addresses fragmentation/PMTUD) --
# neither can fully remove the underlying TCP-over-TCP effect, since the
# Firebox gives no UDP alternative to switch to.
mssfix 1400
# Split-tunnel: only the resources the Firebox pushes via PUSH_REPLY go
# through the VPN. We tried full-tunnel (redirect-gateway def1) and the
# Firebox didn't forward general internet traffic -- it just broke the
# machine's normal connectivity without gaining real browsing.
#
# Fireboxes commonly push broad routes (e.g. 10.0.0.0/8) that can swallow
# local networks that also use those ranges (home WiFi routers, for
# example), breaking the control connection -- and those same broad routes
# sometimes include the Firebox's own public IP, which would break
# reconnection. route-up.sh re-installs more specific routes back to the
# local network AND to the Firebox itself so they coexist without
# stepping on each other (the "save the original route" part is done by
# vpn_routes.py before connecting, not by an --up script, because that
# hook overrode OpenVPN's automatic DNS handling). route-down.sh removes
# that extra host route again on disconnect, so it doesn't linger and
# shadow a different, more specific route to that same IP later.
script-security 2
route-up {{ROUTE_UP}}
route-pre-down {{ROUTE_DOWN}}
